What Is Ansible
Ansible is an open-source automation tool widely used for IT tasks such as

. configuration management

e.g- suppose I want to configure a new service on many systems at a time

. application deployment

e.g- suppose I want to run httpd Apache server and then run my simple application on top of it

. task automation

e.g- suppose I want to clear up my log files of application in servers

It is designed to be

. simple

. agentless

uses SSH for connection

. powerful

enabling users to automate repetitive tasks efficiently. Ansible operates by connecting to nodes (servers or devices) over SSH or WinRM (Windows Remote Management) and executing tasks using YAML-based playbooks.

A Little about architecture
Controller Node: The system where Ansible is installed and from where automation tasks are orchestrated. The controller node manages the inventory of target hosts and executes playbooks to configure and manage them.
>>> Here our Ubuntu 20.04 System

>>> control machine can be only a linux machine

Inventory: The inventory file contains a list of managed nodes that Ansible will work with. It can be static or dynamic, and it can organize hosts into groups to simplify management and orchestration.
Playbooks: Playbooks are written in YAML and define a set of tasks to be executed on managed nodes. They specify the state the system should be in, rather than the exact steps to get there, making them idempotent and easy to understand.
Installation

$ sudo apt update
$ sudo apt install software-properties-common
$ sudo add-apt-repository - yes - update ppa:ansible/ansible
$ sudo apt install ansible
Add the following lines

Have to add utf encoding in /sudo nano /etc/default/locale

LANG="en_US.UTF-8"
LC_CTYPE="en.US.UTF-8"
Then

sudo update-locale LANG=en_US.UTF-8 LC_CTYPE=en_US.UTF-8
Ansible Version

ansible - version
for checking installation

ansible localhost -m ping
